{"type":"text","timestamp":1781046810891,"sessionID":"ses_1515955d6ffegNRB71nAdam01t","part":{"id":"prt_eaea95f1e001MMAX5F4ISPGQNf","messageID":"msg_eaea944b1001Neh6zlBLnpyrdO","sessionID":"ses_1515955d6ffegNRB71nAdam01t","type":"text","text":"<thinking>\nThe user wants me to continue with F1.2: Modify _compute_horizon_signal to return gate_metadata.\n\nLet me carefully plan this change:\n\n1. I need to modify the _compute_horizon_signal function to:\n   - Track which alphas actually passed the gate (separate from gated_alphas which may include fallback)\n   - Build a gate_metadata dict with all required fields\n   - Return it as a 5th element in the return tuple\n\n2. The key insight is that I need to distinguish between:\n   - `gate_passed_alphas`: alphas that actually passed the Research Gate\n   - `gated_alphas`: alphas selected for use (may be gate_passed_alphas OR fallback candidates)\n\nLooking at the code around line 1258-1326:\n- Lines 1258-1264: Call validate_alpha_with_research_gate which returns gated_alphas\n- Line 1268: Compute gate_passed_count\n- Lines 1275-1322: Determine if fallback candidates are used\n- Line 1378: Return statement\n\nThe issue is that `validate_alpha_with_research_gate` at line 772-780 already does a fallback to top_alphas when all alphas fail. So by the time we get `gated_alphas` back, we can't tell if they're actual gate-passing alphas or fallback candidates.\n\nI need to:\n1. Track `gate_passed_alphas` separately - these are alphas that truly passed the gate\n2. Determine `selection_source` based on whether we used fallback candidates or not\n3. Build the metadata dict\n4. Return it\n\nLet me look at the validate_alpha_with_research_gate function more carefully to understand what it returns and where the fallback logic happens.\n\nLooking at lines 758-781:\n```python\ngated_alphas: List[str] = []\nfor alpha in top_alphas:\n    ...\n    if result['passed']:\n        gated_alphas.append(alpha)\n    ...\n\n# Fallback: if all alphas fail gate, use original top_alphas\nif not gated_alphas:\n    logger.warning(\n        \"All alphas failed Research Gate — falling back to top_alphas \"\n        f\"({top_alphas})\"\n    )\n    gated_alphas = list(top_alphas)\n\nlogger.info(f\"Gated alphas ({len(gated_alphas)}/{len(top_alphas)}): {gated_alphas}\")\nreturn gated_alphas, gate_results\n```\n\nSo the issue is that this function does its own fallback. But in the context of P10 gate tuning, we have ANOTHER layer of fallback logic at lines 1280-1322 in _compute_horizon_signal.\n\nI think the cleaner approach is to:\n1. Keep track of which alphas actually passed before the fallback at line 772\n2. In _compute_horizon_signal, determine the selection_source based on used_fallback_candidates flag\n3. Build metadata and return it\n\nActually, I can determine gate_passed_alphas by looking at gate_results:\n```python\ngate_passed_alphas = [alpha for alpha, res in gate_results.items() if _is_gate_verdict_pass(res.get('verdict'))]\n```\n\nThis way I don't need to modify validate_alpha_with_research_gate.\n\nLet me now modify _compute_horizon_signal to add the gate_metadata return value. I'll do this in the section right before the return statement at line 1378.\n</thinking>\n\nI'll now modify `_compute_horizon_signal` to build and return gate_metadata. Let me read the section right before the return statement to understand the context better.","time":{"start":1781046796062,"end":1781046810888}}}
